<Section id="intro" title="Drawing with SVG">

**SVG** (Scalable Vector Graphics) is an image format you write as markup. Instead of storing pixels like a photo, you _describe_ shapes — and because they're vectors, they stay crisp at any size.

Everything we draw lives inside an `<svg>` canvas with a `width` and a `height`. The `class="chart"` just gives every canvas the same background and border.

<BasicSvgDemo />

One thing to keep in mind as you place shapes: the coordinate system starts at the **top-left**, and `y` grows _downward_.

{#snippet sidebar()}

Copy this to start a canvas:

```svelte
/// file: Starter canvas
<svg class="chart" width="700" height="400">
	<rect x="20" y="20" width="160" height="80" fill="tomato" />
</svg>
```

> [!WARNING]
> `(0, 0)` is the **top-left**, not the centre. `y="20"` is 20px _down_ from the top.

{/snippet}

</Section>

<Section id="circles" title="Circles">

Circles are positioned from their **centre** outward. You set where the centre sits with `cx` and `cy`, then give it a radius `r`. Add a `fill` for the inside, and `stroke` / `stroke-width` for the outline.

<CircleDemo />

Because percentages resolve against the canvas, `cx="50%"` keeps a dot centred no matter how the canvas resizes — handy for laying out a few points into a scatter plot.

{#snippet sidebar()}

A single circle:

```svelte
/// file: Circle
<svg class="chart" width="700" height="400">
	<circle cx="100" cy="60" r="40" fill="steelblue" stroke="black" stroke-width="1" />
</svg>
```

> [!WARNING]
> `cx`/`cy` are the **centre**, not a corner — and a circle has no outline unless you set `stroke`.

**End result** — five circles make a scatter plot:

<ScatterResult />

```svelte
/// file: Scatter plot
<svg class="chart" width="700" height="400">
	<circle cx="40%" cy="75%" r="10" fill="steelblue" />
	<circle cx="25%" cy="75%" r="10" fill="steelblue" />
	<circle cx="50%" cy="50%" r="10" fill="steelblue" />
	<circle cx="10%" cy="66%" r="10" fill="steelblue" />
	<circle cx="80%" cy="30%" r="10" fill="steelblue" />
</svg>
```

{/snippet}

</Section>